WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.675
Konstantin Novoselov’s Nobel lecture puts graphene
in a broader family of two dimensional crystals.

00:00:06.725 --> 00:00:09.305
Graphene has one atomic plane.

00:00:09.355 --> 00:00:15.275
A monolayer of molybdenum disulfide has three atomic
planes in a single structural layer.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.435
Take a thin ribbon and pull along its length.

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:24.125
Divide the tensile force by the ribbon width to get
sheet stress in newtons per metre.

00:00:24.175 --> 00:00:28.725
The slope of sheet stress versus strain at small
strain is the sheet modulus.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.170
Lee and colleagues pushed a suspended graphene
membrane with an atomic force microscope.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.930
They fitted the force versus depth curve to infer
stiffness.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.280
As the tip pushes farther, the membrane stretches
geometrically.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:52.350
For a sheet modulus of three hundred forty newtons
per metre and one percent strain, the linear
estimate of sheet stress is three point four newtons
per metre.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.050
Pause and predict the force on a ribbon two
micrometres wide.
